WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.180
Why is P closed under

2
00:00:02.180 --> 00:00:04.620
Kleene star? Trying every cut

3
00:00:04.620 --> 00:00:05.820
of a word is finite

4
00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:08.200
but can be exponential, so

5
00:00:08.200 --> 00:00:10.340
the decidability proof alone is

6
00:00:10.340 --> 00:00:12.560
not enough. We will replace

7
00:00:12.560 --> 00:00:15.160
that enumeration with prefix dynamic

8
00:00:15.160 --> 00:00:17.640
programming and count both membership

9
00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:19.080
calls and the work of

10
00:00:19.080 --> 00:00:20.140
carrying them out.

11
00:00:20.460 --> 00:00:23.200
A greedy longest-prefix method fails

12
00:00:23.700 --> 00:00:25.100
even for a tiny finite

13
00:00:25.100 --> 00:00:27.780
language. Let L contain zero,

14
00:00:28.020 --> 00:00:29.100
zero one and one one,

15
00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:30.420
and take the input zero

16
00:00:30.660 --> 00:00:33.100
one one. Greedy removes zero

17
00:00:33.340 --> 00:00:34.280
one and gets stuck on

18
00:00:34.280 --> 00:00:36.160
one, although the valid factorization

19
00:00:36.160 --> 00:00:38.040
is zero followed by one

20
00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:38.320
one.

21
00:00:38.740 --> 00:00:40.600
Define D of j to

22
00:00:40.600 --> 00:00:41.920
mean the first j symbols

23
00:00:41.920 --> 00:00:43.780
belong to L star. Set

24
00:00:43.780 --> 00:00:45.740
D of zero true because

25
00:00:45.740 --> 00:00:47.060
the empty prefix is a

26
00:00:47.060 --> 00:00:49.580
concatenation of zero factors. For

27
00:00:49.580 --> 00:00:50.880
a positive j, look for

28
00:00:50.880 --> 00:00:52.260
a smaller i with D

29
00:00:52.260 --> 00:00:53.780
of i true and the

30
00:00:53.780 --> 00:00:55.500
substring from i plus one

31
00:00:55.500 --> 00:00:56.600
through j in L.

32
00:00:57.340 --> 00:00:59.640
For zero one one, D

33
00:00:59.640 --> 00:01:01.560
of one is true because

34
00:01:01.560 --> 00:01:03.720
its one-symbol prefix zero belongs

35
00:01:03.720 --> 00:01:05.100
to L. D of two

36
00:01:05.100 --> 00:01:07.460
is also true because zero

37
00:01:07.720 --> 00:01:09.980
one belongs to L. These

38
00:01:09.980 --> 00:01:11.940
are valid prefix facts, not

39
00:01:11.940 --> 00:01:13.260
a commitment to using the

40
00:01:13.260 --> 00:01:15.140
longer prefix in every later

41
00:01:15.140 --> 00:01:15.980
factorization.

42
00:01:16.420 --> 00:01:17.980
To compute D of three,

43
00:01:18.300 --> 00:01:18.960
the whole word zero one

44
00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:20.700
one is not itself in

45
00:01:20.700 --> 00:01:21.960
L. But split after the

46
00:01:21.960 --> 00:01:23.580
first symbol: D of one

47
00:01:23.580 --> 00:01:24.760
is true and the final

48
00:01:24.760 --> 00:01:25.920
one one is in L.

49
00:01:26.220 --> 00:01:27.760
Therefore D of three becomes

50
00:01:27.760 --> 00:01:30.380
true, recovering the factorization the

51
00:01:30.380 --> 00:01:31.720
greedy algorithm missed.

52
00:01:32.220 --> 00:01:33.920
Prove the recurrence by induction

53
00:01:33.920 --> 00:01:35.920
on prefix length. If a

54
00:01:35.920 --> 00:01:38.360
tested split succeeds, append its

55
00:01:38.360 --> 00:01:40.060
last L-factor to a valid

56
00:01:40.060 --> 00:01:43.500
earlier prefix factorization. Conversely, a

57
00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:45.100
nonempty word in L star

58
00:01:45.100 --> 00:01:46.740
has a factorization whose empty

59
00:01:46.740 --> 00:01:48.600
factors can be discarded, so

60
00:01:48.600 --> 00:01:50.420
its final nonempty factor begins

61
00:01:50.420 --> 00:01:51.580
at one of the tested

62
00:01:51.580 --> 00:01:52.480
smaller indices.

63
00:01:53.540 --> 00:01:55.420
The algorithm accepts an empty

64
00:01:55.420 --> 00:01:57.880
input immediately, whether or not

65
00:01:57.880 --> 00:01:59.820
epsilon belongs to L. For

66
00:01:59.820 --> 00:02:02.640
nonempty inputs, excluding empty factors

67
00:02:02.640 --> 00:02:04.900
ensures each predecessor index is

68
00:02:04.900 --> 00:02:07.580
strictly smaller. Allowing infinitely many

69
00:02:07.580 --> 00:02:09.460
empty factors would add no

70
00:02:09.460 --> 00:02:11.080
new words and would obscure

71
00:02:11.080 --> 00:02:13.680
this finite dynamic-programming structure.

72
00:02:14.300 --> 00:02:15.760
There is at most one

73
00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:17.600
membership call per index pair

74
00:02:17.600 --> 00:02:19.080
with zero at most i

75
00:02:19.080 --> 00:02:20.500
less than j at most

76
00:02:20.500 --> 00:02:22.760
n. Summing j from one

77
00:02:22.760 --> 00:02:24.360
through n gives n times

78
00:02:24.360 --> 00:02:25.860
n plus one over two

79
00:02:25.860 --> 00:02:28.060
calls. For the three-symbol example

80
00:02:28.060 --> 00:02:29.240
that is at most six

81
00:02:29.240 --> 00:02:31.600
calls, although short-circuiting may make

82
00:02:31.600 --> 00:02:31.980
fewer.

83
00:02:32.340 --> 00:02:34.100
Suppose the given decider takes

84
00:02:34.100 --> 00:02:35.320
time Big O of m

85
00:02:35.320 --> 00:02:37.140
to c on nonempty inputs,

86
00:02:37.140 --> 00:02:38.720
with c at least one.

87
00:02:39.240 --> 00:02:40.880
Each tested substring has length

88
00:02:40.880 --> 00:02:42.460
at most n, and copying,

89
00:02:42.460 --> 00:02:44.760
simulation cleanup and table access

90
00:02:44.760 --> 00:02:46.440
can be bounded by polynomial

91
00:02:46.440 --> 00:02:48.000
scans as in the tutorial

92
00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:50.320
implementation. With order n squared

93
00:02:50.320 --> 00:02:52.240
tests, the stated multitape bound

94
00:02:52.240 --> 00:02:53.360
is Big O of n

95
00:02:53.360 --> 00:02:54.380
to c plus two.

96
00:02:54.780 --> 00:02:56.080
Things to take note of.

97
00:02:56.440 --> 00:02:57.820
State what the DP table

98
00:02:57.820 --> 00:03:00.300
means, prove both recurrence directions

99
00:03:00.300 --> 00:03:02.340
and include the empty input.

100
00:03:02.980 --> 00:03:05.220
Membership calls are real computations

101
00:03:05.220 --> 00:03:07.480
rather than unit-cost magic, and

102
00:03:07.480 --> 00:03:09.360
a finite exhaustive partition search

103
00:03:09.360 --> 00:03:11.500
proves decidability without proving the

104
00:03:11.500 --> 00:03:13.360
stronger polynomial-time claim.
